Session 10 summary — relay boundary reached, state fully saved (state.md + two ccmemory dossiers + awareness docs).

**Answer to the criteria ("is it production ready?"): No** — but this session closed the biggest open defect and got the entire TCP transport matrix green.

**Root-caused and fixed (v0.11.104→108, srcver B2A39830):**

1. **Shape-1 coherency family CLOSED.** Three live captures proved `xfs_lookup` was returning reused-inode-number inodes whose in-place reload had silently bailed under contention: the type-flip flavor made every walk fail ENOTDIR for ~1s (silently lost creates — the uv census 127/128), and the same-type flavor served stale size-0 shells (empty reads). The smoking gun was sitting in the node shell logs the whole time ("Not a directory"). Fix: the lookup now block-retries the reload (≤2s, converges in 1 round) instead of handing the VFS an unconverged incarnation. Verified: **40 consecutive cache_coherency@16 PASS** (pre-fix ~2-in-7 fail), with the fix arms firing constantly and resolving every time.
2. **dlm_scaling@32 collapse fixed** (15→34 ops/s): the last synchronous per-unlink disk read (46-123ms queue-wait) is now skipped when the node holds local authority (LOCAL_UNLINK + EX), mirroring the existing sess7 nlink-skip precedent.
3. **Deployment integrity (nasty one):** nodes were sometimes loading *frankenstein* mxfs.ko images — mixed stale/new NFS pages with a matching srcversion but old code (proven by `strings` on the loaded ko). That was the real cause of both withdraw-recovery "completion never fired" anomalies. The prep now copies the module node-local and md5-verifies against the build host before insmod. Withdraw test passes at 16 and 32 since.

**Board status at single srcver, verified deployment: 4/tcp 19/19, 8/tcp 20/20, 16/tcp 20/20 in-chunk, 32/tcp all rows pass standalone** (32-node chunked runs saturate clyde at load 40-64 — dispositioned as host capacity, real 32-scale belongs to the physical rig).

**Why not production ready:** the first-ever CAW-transport run on this LIO rig is catastrophically broken (cross-visibility failures everywhere, CAW EX publish failures, one node shutdown) — historical CAW validation was all SCST, so next session starts with `tools/caw_verify` against the LIO LUN before suspecting mxfs code. Also outstanding: caw/cawd portal infrastructure, 2/1-node boards, and the physical-rig matrix — the noisy boxes behind you stay relevant only for that final phase; they can remain powered off until then.

